Mark externally stored ezm7 tensors as external data.

ezm7EncodeWithExternalStore writes the payload to the external store but
doesn't set 0x1000_0000 in the identifier (unlike other external encoders).

Readers then decode the 16-byte pointer as an inline ezm7 payload and
obtain zeros, or garbage, or even crash due to out-of-bounds memory access.
If you then recompact the store with TensorData.makeCompactStore, it skips
the row and loses it forever.
